course: reflection
lesson: 5
title: When it was my fault: owning up and making it right
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Sometimes the bad thing really was partly your fault, and the honest job is to own your part without drowning in it: not "it's nothing to do with me" and not "I'm a horrible person", but "I did this, and here is how I'll put it right".
explain: Noah's dad asked him to watch his little sister in the park while he took a call; Noah played on his phone, she fell off the climbing frame and broke her arm, and for weeks the sight of her cast made him feel sick: "If I'd been watching, she wouldn't have fallen."
explain: Both things were true at once: accidents can happen even when someone is watching, and Dad himself said a nine-year-old should not have been left in charge so long; but Noah also learned to put the phone away when he is looking after someone.
explain: Guilt is useful when it tells you to care more next time, and useless when it just tells you to hate yourself every night; helping his sister while her arm healed did more for both of them than lying awake.
explain: Owning up early, even with a shaky voice ("I was too scared", "I was being lazy"), is braver than an invented excuse, and it usually makes the weight lighter.
fact: Owning up means saying honestly what your part was; it is usually braver and works out better than inventing an excuse.
fact: Accidents can happen even when people are watching, but not paying attention is still something to learn from.
fact: Useful guilt tells you to care more next time; it is not meant to make you hate yourself.
fact: Promises matter even when you are tired; showing up for people is one of the biggest ways to show you care, and friendship works both ways.
fact: Secrets about someone's feelings, like who they have a crush on, are especially private; what is funny to one person can be painful to another.
fact: Older people often feel lonely, and a short regular phone call can mean a lot to them; it is never too late to start calling again.
fact: Fear can make people quit; saying "I was too scared" is braver than pretending to be ill, and telling someone early gives them time to help.
fact: Forgetting things is normal; a system such as a notes page or a reminder board on the fridge helps more than blaming yourself.
fact: Bad moods can make us mean even to people we like; noticing it and making up for it is how we get kinder.
fact: When a secret is not dangerous, it is not ours to share; when it is about someone being hurt or in danger, telling a trusted adult is right.
fact: Little children remember being left out; giving them time and attention later is what makes it up to them.
fact: A near miss is when something bad almost happens; the brain keeps replaying "what could have happened" as a warning to be careful, not as a punishment.
exercise: Noah was on his phone when his little sister fell and broke her arm. Which thought is the fairest? | I wasn't watching and I'll learn from that, but accidents can happen anyway | It is completely my fault and I should feel awful about it every single day forever | It had nothing at all to do with me, so there is nothing to learn | It was all my sister's fault for climbing so high in the park
exercise: What helped Noah most? | Dad saying he left her too long, and helping his sister | Blaming himself every night until the cast came off his sister's arm | Pretending he had been watching her the whole time | Staying away from his sister so he didn't see the cast
exercise: Jess skipped her friend's dance show because she was tired, though her friend came to all her football matches. What did Jess say that helped? | "I'm sorry I didn't come, I was being lazy" | "I had to go to hospital, it was an emergency" | "Your show was probably boring anyway, so it didn't matter" | Nothing; she just waited for her friend to forget
exercise: Finn told his friend's crush secret "as a joke" and the crush laughed. What is true? | What is funny to one person can be painful to another | It was just a joke, so nobody has any right to be upset | Crush secrets don't count, so it's fine to share them | His friend should have laughed too, to be a good sport
exercise: Isla stopped her Sunday calls to Nan, and then Nan got ill. What is the best thing to do now? | Call Nan, say she thought about her, and start the Sunday calls again | Stay away from Nan, because it is far too late to start calling her again now | Wait until Nan calls first to show she isn't angry | Feel terrible every day so Nan knows she is sorry
exercise: Dylan quit the band two days before the concert because he was nervous and pretended to be ill. What would have been braver? | Telling the band teacher he was too scared | Pretending to have an even worse illness on the day | Turning up and playing badly on purpose to spoil it | Blaming another band member for the gap in the music
exercise: Maddie forgot to give Mum the school trip letter and missed the trip. What helped her most? | A reminder board on the fridge | Blaming herself for weeks afterwards | Promising to try harder without any plan | Telling Mum the teacher never gave it to her
exercise: Archie wouldn't lend his bike because he was in a mood, and his friend walked home in the rain. What did Archie learn? | Bad moods can make us mean, and noticing that helps us be kinder | Lending things is always a mistake between friends | It was the friend's own fault for not owning a bike of his own to ride | Being in a mood means you can't control anything
exercise: Leah told Mum her friend's parents were splitting up, and Mum told another mum. What should Leah do? | Tell her friend the truth, say sorry, and ask Mum to stop sharing | Hide it and hope her friend never finds out who told | Blame her mum completely and tell her friend she had no part in it | Tell more people so that it's no longer a secret
exercise: When is it right to tell a grown-up a friend's secret? | When it's about someone being hurt or in danger | Whenever the secret is interesting enough to share | Never, even if your friend is in danger | Only when the friend is not around to hear
exercise: Kian left his six-year-old cousin for the older kids in the three-legged race, and his cousin cried. What fixed it? | Spending a whole afternoon playing with his cousin | Telling him he's too little to understand big kids' games | Buying him a toy instead of spending any time with him | Saying the three-legged race was stupid and babyish anyway
exercise: Sofia let go of the dog lead to check her phone and the dog ran into the road. He was fine. Why does she keep picturing what could have happened? | The brain replays a near miss as a warning to be careful | She is going mad and needs to forget the dog | It means the accident will definitely happen for real next time | The brain only replays things that really happened
exercise: What changed for Sofia after her near miss? | Phone in her pocket on walks and road training with Dad | She never walked the dog again in her life | She decided phones are fine on walks after all, as the dog was fine | She blamed the driver who shouted at her
exercise: Which is the best order when something was your fault? | Own up, say sorry, and make a plan to fix it | Wait to be caught, then quickly make up a good excuse | Say sorry, then go on and do the same thing again | Blame yourself forever, but change nothing at all
test: Your mate goes, "nah, I just couldn't be arsed going to her show." ("Couldn't be arsed" is a rude way to say couldn't be bothered.) She came to all his matches. What's the honest view? | Being tired is real, but she showed up for him, so he should say sorry | He's right, friends never need to show up for each other's shows or games | It doesn't matter, because shows are only for girls | He should invent a story about being ill
test: What would happen if Dylan told the band teacher a week early, "I'm scared of the stage"? | The teacher could help him or fill the gap in time | He'd be thrown out of school for being scared | Nobody would ever trust him with music again | It would make his stage fright much worse forever
test: Finn's cousin from Texas says, "y'all are makin' a big deal, it was a joke." What should Finn say to his friend instead of "it was just a joke"? | A proper sorry, and then keeping his friend's secrets after that | "Stop being so sensitive, everyone laughs at crushes, it's just a laugh" | Nothing, because jokes never need a sorry | "Tell me another secret and I'll keep that one"
test: Nan's mate, all Belfast: "Ach, she'd love to hear from ye, just a wee call." ("Wee" means short or small.) Isla is scared it's too late. Which is true? | It's never too late; a short call can mean a lot to a lonely gran | It's too late, Nan will never forgive her | She should send Nan a nice present instead of calling, because it's easier | Only long calls of an hour count to grandparents
test: In a TV show, a kid breaks something and the camera shows him hiding the evidence and getting away with it, all happy. What's unrealistic? | Hiding things usually makes guilt grow, not disappear | Nothing; hiding things always makes people happy | In real life nobody ever breaks anything | Real kids always get caught within one minute
test: Maddie's gran calls her a "proper scatterbrain" and says "my head's like a sieve too." What's the useful lesson? | Forgetting is normal, and a system like a fridge board helps | Scatterbrains can never learn to remember things | She should practise remembering without any help | Only old people are allowed to forget things
test: Archie's mate, Aussie, gets home soaked: "cheers for nothing, mate, walked home in the rain." What does Archie do next time that shows he's learned? | Lends the bike without being asked | Says "it's your own fault, buy a bike" | Hides his bike so nobody can ask to borrow it | Lends it next time, but charges him money for it
test: A kid says, "My bestie told me a secret: her stepdad hits her, but I swore not to tell." What should she do? | Tell a trusted adult, because someone being hurt is not a secret to keep | Keep the promise, because a promise always comes first | Tell the whole class so everyone can help | Ask the stepdad to stop by herself
test: Kian's aunt says he "left the wee one hanging" at the picnic. ("Left him hanging" means left him without help.) What makes it up to his cousin? | Time and attention, like playing with him all afternoon | Telling him he'll understand when he's older | A quick "sorry" while running back to the big kids | Nothing; little kids forget everything by the next day
test: Noah's friend goes, "Your sister broke her arm cos of you, you're the worst brother ever." What's the fairer view? | He learns to watch, but accidents can happen anyway | His friend is right, he is the worst brother ever | It's his sister's fault for climbing too high | Nobody ever bears any part of an accident
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of guilt following an error? | It serves to encourage greater care on future occasions | It serves as a penalty to be endured for the remainder of one's life | It indicates that the person concerned is incapable of reform | It is without any useful function whatsoever
exam: Identify the course of conduct most consistent with integrity where a pupil has withdrawn from a commitment owing to fear. | Candid acknowledgement of the fear to the responsible adult | Fabrication of an illness in order to avoid embarrassment | Attribution of the withdrawal to another participant | Silence pending the discovery of the absence by others
exam: Given that a confidence entrusted by a friend concerns no danger to any person, determine the appropriate course of action. | The confidence is not one's own to disclose and should be kept | The confidence may be disclosed to any adult without restriction | The confidence should be disclosed to the whole class | The confidence should be exchanged for another confidence
exam: Ascertain which of the following describes a "near miss". | An occurrence in which harm almost, but did not, result | An occurrence in which grave harm has certainly resulted | An occurrence wholly incapable of causing any harm | An occurrence that shall inevitably recur on the next occasion
